          "id": "And for you?",
          "literal": "and — you?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Андрій:",
          "text": "[Мені|Meh-NEE] [чай|chai] [і|ee] [борщ|borshch].",
          "id": "Tea and borscht for me.",
          "literal": "to-me — tea — and — borscht."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Офіціантка:",
          "text": "[Борщ|Borshch] [зі|zee] [сметаною|smeh-TA-no-yoo]?",
          "id": "Borscht with sour cream?",
          "literal": "borscht — with — sour-cream (орудний)?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Андрій:",
          "text": "[Так|Tak], [будь|bood'] [ласка|LAS-ka]. [І|EE] [хліб|khleeb].",
          "id": "Yes, please. And some bread.",
          "literal": "yes, be — kindness. and — bread."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Офіціантка:",
          "text": "[Щось|Shchos'] [на|na] [десерт|deh-SERT]?",
          "id": "Anything for dessert?",
          "literal": "something — for — dessert?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ані:",
          "text": "[Так|Tak], [одне|od-NEH] [морозиво|mo-RO-zi-vo]. [І|EE] [рахунок|ra-KHOO-nok] [разом|RA-zom], [будь|bood'] [ласка|LAS-ka].",
          "id": "Yes, one ice cream. And one bill for both of us, please.",
          "literal": "yes, one (n) — ice-cream. and — bill — together, be — kindness."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Офіціантка:",
          "text": "[Добре|DOB-reh]. [Хвилинку|Khvi-LIN-koo].",
          "id": "Sure. Just a moment.",
          "literal": "good. a-little-minute (знахідний)."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Андрій:",
          "text": "[Смачного|Smach-NO-ho]!",
          "id": "Enjoy your meal!",
          "literal": "tasty (родовий)!"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**Мені, будь ласка — the safest ordering formula.** Literally \"to me, please\". The verb is dropped entirely, so you don't have to choose between *хочу* (I want), *буду* (I'll have), or *візьму* (I'll take). Every server in Ukraine understands it.",
        "**Що ви будете? literally means \"what will you be?\"** The verb is left hanging without an object — much like English \"What'll it be?\". The most natural answer follows the same shape: *Я буду каву* (I'll have a coffee).",
        "**What you order goes into the знахідний, which means its ending changes — sometimes.** Feminine nouns in **-а** switch to **-у**: *кава* → *каву*, *вода* → *воду*, *піца* → *піцу*. Inanimate masculine nouns and neuter nouns **don't change at all**: *чай*, *борщ*, *хліб*, *молоко*, *морозиво*.",
        "**That's why the знахідний often feels invisible.** Half the nouns you'll order happen not to change shape. Don't be fooled — the case is still there, and it becomes very real as soon as you talk about someone alive (Unit 13) or a direction (Unit 13 too).",
        "**З takes the орудний, без takes the родовий.** *З молоком* (with milk), *без молока* (without milk) — the same word with two different endings. This pair comes up with every coffee order in Ukraine, so it's worth learning whole: *з цукром / без цукру*, *зі сметаною / без сметани*.",
        "**Смачного isn't literally \"enjoy your meal\".** It's a fragment of \"(I wish you something) tasty\", which is why it's in the родовий. You say it to someone about to eat — servers say it as they bring the plate, and friends say it before the first bite.",
        "**Рахунок разом or окремо.** *Разом* = together, *окремо* = separately. Paying separately is very common in Ukraine and not considered stingy at all. The full sentence: *Рахунок, будь ласка, окремо.*"
      ],
